diff --git a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
index e88543cbf..82c85df54 100644
--- a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
+++ b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
@@ -266,6 +266,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - Avoid settling on bonuses (especially food/metal/high-production/commerce) unless the immediate overall site value clearly justifies it. See [KI#25](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#25---seemingly-fixed-ais-settling-too-much-and-too-often-on-bonuses-especially-food-bonuses--and-metals-and-other-high-production-bonuses-or-also-commerce-bonuses-to-a-lesser-extent).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5929+) **Unowned bonus extra valuation in city-site valuation:** for example, if we already own Wheat, a site with 1 Maize is **much better for the whole empire** than a city site with 2 more Wheat, which would have **more yields locally for this city** (e.g., ~+3-4 extra food per plot), **but much less long-term empire growth** (e.g., +1 Health +1 Health with Granary for all cities) (which is perhaps better even for this local city). Or, stone, happiness, or other bonuses could provide other advantages, for example **trading, military, accelerating buildables, etc**. Trade imports do not count as owned because they are not permanent. Applied once per bonus type and only after the first city. See [KI#147](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#147---fixedenhanced-base-advciv-issue-of-ai-settlers-not-adding-extra-valuation-to-unowned-bonuses-in-city-site-scoring).
 - **Settler site eval - devalue BFCs with many very bad tiles:** Candidate sites are **significantly devalued** (not auto-rejected) when (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5977+) enough non-home BFC plots are impassable or remain very low-yield after their best XML-valid improvement: the threshold as of now is dynamic `4 * food + 2 * production + commerce` (e.g., 1 food is not very bad, 1 hammer and 2 commerce is very bad, peak is very bad) (visible bonuses on usable plots are not counted because bonuses are likely to be always valuable). See [KI#26.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#262---enhanced-ai-settlers-not-devaluing-very-bad-non-home-tiles-peak-ice-cap-non-hill-desert-without-a-bonus-non-hill-snow-without-a-bonus-if-we-have-enough-of-them-in-bfc).
+- **First-city Good enough plots threshold**: AI settlers now also require the first-city site to have **enough** (e.g., >=13) **“good enough”** (i.e. tile is not impassable, not persistently barren by yield-potential logic, or is a reachable/already-improved water bonus) **BFC tiles** before accepting it, so the first-city is less likely to be founded on a long-term disadvantageous location.
 - **Respect water bonuses:** if a coast bonus would be in BFC but the site is non-coastal, apply a stronger penalty so we don’t "count" unusable sea resources. Penalty moved from **-165 → -400** (K-Mod used ~-800).
 - **Low-food environments**: (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5976+) evaluate food scarcity from each non-home BFC plot's natural food and food added by bonus-specific improvements (e.g., tundra is low food, peak is very low food, but if there are bonuses that give food in the site it reduces the net food-scarcity score (e.g., high tundra high fish/deer may be better than grass/plain hybrid no food from bonuses so do not discard it)). When an inland site has too much net food scarcity, **prefer coastal sites** so the city can grow instead of remaining at very low population. See [KI#32](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#32---now-seemingly-fixed-prioritize-settling-on-coast-when-food-environment-is-low-many-tundra-orand-plains-orand-desert-orand-snow-orand-peak-although-i-assume-is-not-counted-but-to-be-safe-the-corinth-screenshot).
 - **Environment heuristics (non-bonus tiles):** favor sites with lots of grassland/flood plains; penalize plains/desert BFCs. For the tile you settle on, prefer hill-plains/tundra/desert; avoid settling on hill-grass/flood plains unless doing so unlocks a clearly superior multi-bonus BFC. See [KI#26](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#26---seemingly-fixed-for-the-plot-of-the-settled-city-only-in-general-the-ai-should-aim-to-settle-on-a-hill-and-avoid-settling-on-flatland-grassland-or-flood-plains-where-possible-or-during-the-first-city---city-1-when-there-is-no-good-site-yet-to-take).

